IMPORT prelude

-- Small Claims Tribunals Act 1984 (Singapore) -- ss 2, 5 to 10 and the
-- Schedule: what the tribunal can hear, and what happens to a claim that is
-- too big, too old or already somewhere else.
--
-- Unlike the Employment Claims Act 2016, this Act states its own money limits:
-- s 2(1) fixes the prescribed limit at $20,000 and the prescribed extended
-- limit at $30,000, each subject to variation by order in the Gazette after
-- consultation with the Chief Justice. No such order was retrieved, so the
-- limits are carried as parameters and the Act's own figures are supplied.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/SCTA1984.txt. The deposit carries Act 12 of
-- 2020 amendments to the Schedule with effect from 1 October 2025.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules
-- skill. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human
-- gate.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: ss 3, 4, 13 and 14 (which constitute the tribunals and the
-- Registry), ss 12, 15 to 33 (the procedure, the Registrar's consultation
-- powers, referral to a Community Mediation Centre, service, representation,
-- evidence and adjournment), ss 43 to 45, and s 47 (rules). No rules under s 47
-- were retrieved.

§ `Small Claims Tribunals Act 1984 -- jurisdiction`

§§ `2(1) and the Schedule -- what counts as a specified claim`

-- s 5(1)(a) confines the tribunal to a "specified claim", and s 2(1) defines
-- that as "a claim specified in the Schedule". The Schedule's paragraph 1 lists
-- nine.
--
-- Worth setting out who each limb is for. Limbs (a), (b) and (c) are the ones an
-- individual uses. The other six are all claims BY an institution FOR the
-- recovery of money from an individual: an owner developer, a management
-- corporation, the Housing and Development Board, the Council of the Singapore
-- Business Federation, and a Town Council twice over. Two thirds of the
-- tribunal's subject-matter jurisdiction exists so that bodies can collect.
--
-- Schedule para 1(b) excludes "a claim mentioned in section 4 of the Community
-- Disputes Resolution Act 2015" -- the neighbour-nuisance claims, which go to
-- the Community Disputes Resolution Tribunals instead.
--
-- Schedule para 2 deems a contract to buy or sell foreign currency notes made
-- with a licensed money-changer to be a contract for the provision of services,
-- and so within para 1(a).
DECLARE ClaimSubject IS ONE OF
    `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
    `a contract to buy or sell foreign currency notes with a licensed money-changer`
    `a tort claim for damage caused to property`
    `a claim mentioned in section 4 of the Community Disputes Resolution Act 2015`
    `a contract for the lease of residential premises not exceeding 2 years`
    `a contract for the lease of residential premises exceeding 2 years`
    `an owner developer recovering a charge or interest under the Building (Strata Management) Act`
    `a management corporation recovering a contribution or interest under that Act`
    `the Housing and Development Board recovering an improvement contribution, interest or penalty`
    `the Council of the Singapore Business Federation recovering a subscription`
    `a Town Council recovering an improvement contribution, interest or penalty`
    `a Town Council recovering a charge, fee, expense or penalty`
    `something the Schedule does not specify`

GIVEN `the subject` IS A ClaimSubject
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this is a specified claim` IF
    CONSIDER `the subject`
    WHEN `a claim mentioned in section 4 of the Community Disputes Resolution Act 2015` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a contract for the lease of residential premises exceeding 2 years` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `something the Schedule does not specify` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- Schedule para 1(a) read with para 2: the money-changer contract is deemed a
-- contract for services, so it is a specified claim by that route and not
-- because the Schedule names it separately.
GIVEN `the subject` IS A ClaimSubject
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the claim is within Schedule paragraph 1(a)` IF
    CONSIDER `the subject`
    WHEN `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a contract to buy or sell foreign currency notes with a licensed money-changer` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 35(1)(g) attaches only to a claim under Schedule para 1(c), so that limb
-- needs to be identifiable on its own.
GIVEN `the subject` IS A ClaimSubject
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the claim is within Schedule paragraph 1(c)` IF
    CONSIDER `the subject`
    WHEN `a contract for the lease of residential premises not exceeding 2 years` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The division of the Schedule between individuals and collecting institutions.
GIVEN `the subject` IS A ClaimSubject
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this limb of the Schedule exists for an institution to collect` IF
    CONSIDER `the subject`
    WHEN `an owner developer recovering a charge or interest under the Building (Strata Management) Act` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a management corporation recovering a contribution or interest under that Act` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Housing and Development Board recovering an improvement contribution, interest or penalty` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Council of the Singapore Business Federation recovering a subscription` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Town Council recovering an improvement contribution, interest or penalty` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Town Council recovering a charge, fee, expense or penalty` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `2(2) -- the value of a contract claim`

-- s 2(2): the value of a claim relating to a contract means --
--   (a) where the nature of the claim is RESCISSION of the contract -- THE VALUE
--       OF THE CONTRACT;
--   (b) where it is for the recovery of a PROGRESS PAYMENT due under the
--       contract -- THE VALUE OF THE CONTRACT; and
--   (c) in any other case -- the quantum of the claim.
--
-- Limbs (a) and (b) are the trap. A claimant who has paid a $4,000 deposit under
-- a $90,000 renovation contract and wants out has a claim valued at $90,000, not
-- $4,000 -- so it is outside the prescribed limit, outside the extended limit,
-- and cannot be brought here even by abandoning the excess, because there is no
-- excess to abandon: what exceeds the limit is the value of the contract, not
-- the sum sought. The same claimant suing for the $4,000 as a debt is inside the
-- limit on limb (c).
DECLARE NatureOfContractClaim IS ONE OF
    `rescission of the contract`
    `recovery of a progress payment due under the contract`
    `anything else`

DECLARE ClaimValue HAS
    `the claim relates to a contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the nature of the contract claim` IS A NatureOfContractClaim
    `the value of the contract, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `the quantum of the claim, in dollars` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN `the value` IS A ClaimValue
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the value of the claim` IS
    IF NOT `the value`'s `the claim relates to a contract`
    THEN `the value`'s `the quantum of the claim, in dollars`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the value`'s `the nature of the contract claim`
         WHEN `rescission of the contract` THEN `the value`'s `the value of the contract, in dollars`
         WHEN `recovery of a progress payment due under the contract` THEN `the value`'s `the value of the contract, in dollars`
         OTHERWISE `the value`'s `the quantum of the claim, in dollars`

§§ `5 -- jurisdiction`

-- s 5(1): "Subject to the provisions of this Act, a tribunal has the jurisdiction
-- to hear and determine any claim which -- (a) is a specified claim; and (b) is
-- SERVED IN SINGAPORE on the respondent under section 19(1)."
--
-- Limb (b) makes service in Singapore a condition of jurisdiction and not merely
-- of procedure. A respondent who cannot be served here is beyond the tribunal
-- whatever the claim is worth.
--
-- s 5(2): the jurisdiction "does not extend to a claim --
--   (a) in respect of DAMAGE CAUSED TO ANY PROPERTY BY AN ACCIDENT arising out
--       of or in connection with the USE OF A MOTOR VEHICLE; or
--   (b) which the State Courts have no jurisdiction to hear and determine."
--
-- Limb (a) carves a large hole out of Schedule para 1(b). A dented car, a
-- scraped gate, a broken wing mirror -- the commonest small property claim there
-- is -- is outside the tribunal and belongs in the Magistrate's Court, with that
-- court's costs and procedure.
--
-- s 5(3): "EXCEPT WHERE THIS ACT EXPRESSLY PROVIDES OTHERWISE, the jurisdiction
-- of a tribunal does not extend to a claim --
--   (a) the value of which exceeds the prescribed limit; or
--   (b) AFTER THE EXPIRY OF 2 YEARS after the date on which the cause of action
--       accrued."
--
-- s 5(4): "SUBJECT TO SUBSECTIONS (1) AND (2), a tribunal has jurisdiction to
-- hear and determine any claim the value of which exceeds the prescribed limit
-- but does not exceed the prescribed extended limit, IF THE PARTIES TO THE CLAIM
-- SO AGREE BY A MEMORANDUM SIGNED BY THEM."
--
-- s 5(5): where the tribunal "is of the opinion that a claim lodged with it ...
-- is beyond its jurisdiction, the tribunal MUST DISCONTINUE the proceedings and
-- advise the claimant and respondent accordingly."
DECLARE Limits HAS
    `the prescribed limit` IS A NUMBER
    `the prescribed extended limit` IS A NUMBER

-- s 2(1) states both figures, each "or such other sum as the Minister may, after
-- consultation with the Chief Justice, prescribe by order in the Gazette". No
-- order was retrieved.
`the limits stated in section 2(1)` MEANS Limits WITH
    `the prescribed limit` IS 20000
    `the prescribed extended limit` IS 30000

DECLARE Claim HAS
    `the subject` IS A ClaimSubject
    `the value` IS A ClaimValue
    `served in Singapore on the respondent under section 19(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `in respect of damage to property by an accident involving the use of a motor vehicle` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the State Courts have no jurisdiction over it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `years since the cause of action accrued` IS A NUMBER
    `the parties have agreed the extended limit by a memorandum signed by them` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the claimant has abandoned the excess under section 9` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5(1) is satisfied` IF
        `this is a specified claim` (`the claim`'s `the subject`)
    AND `the claim`'s `served in Singapore on the respondent under section 19(1)`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5(2) excludes the claim` IF
        `the claim`'s `in respect of damage to property by an accident involving the use of a motor vehicle`
    OR  `the claim`'s `the State Courts have no jurisdiction over it`

-- s 5(3)(a), and the two things that expressly provide otherwise about value:
-- the memorandum under s 5(4) and the abandonment under s 9(1).
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the value is within the prescribed limit` IF
    `the value of the claim` (`the claim`'s `the value`) AT MOST `the limits`'s `the prescribed limit`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the value is within the prescribed extended limit` IF
    `the value of the claim` (`the claim`'s `the value`) AT MOST `the limits`'s `the prescribed extended limit`

-- s 5(4) operates only in the band between the two limits, and only with the
-- respondent's signature. A respondent facing a claim in that band and happy to
-- be sued in the Magistrate's Court instead simply declines to sign.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5(4) applies to the claim` IF
        NOT `the value is within the prescribed limit` `the claim` `the limits`
    AND `the value is within the prescribed extended limit` `the claim` `the limits`
    AND `the claim`'s `the parties have agreed the extended limit by a memorandum signed by them`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the value requirement is met` IF
        `the value is within the prescribed limit` `the claim` `the limits`
    OR  `section 5(4) applies to the claim` `the claim` `the limits`
    OR  `the claim`'s `the claimant has abandoned the excess under section 9`

-- s 5(3)(b): two years from accrual. The general limitation period for a claim
-- in contract or tort is six years, so a claimant with a stale small claim is
-- out of this tribunal and still inside the ordinary courts -- where s 46
-- expressly leaves them free to go. The cheap forum has the short clock.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the claim is within the two-year period in section 5(3)(b)` IF
    `the claim`'s `years since the cause of action accrued` AT MOST 2

§§§ `The reading question in section 5(3) and section 5(4)`

-- s 5(3) begins "EXCEPT WHERE THIS ACT EXPRESSLY PROVIDES OTHERWISE" and then
-- states two bars: the value bar in (a) and the two-year bar in (b).
--
-- s 5(4) is a freestanding conferral of jurisdiction -- "a tribunal HAS
-- jurisdiction to hear and determine any claim the value of which ..." -- and it
-- is expressed to be "SUBJECT TO SUBSECTIONS (1) AND (2)". Not to subsection
-- (3).
--
-- Read literally, s 5(4) is a provision that expressly provides otherwise, and
-- it is not subject to s 5(3) at all. So a memorandum under s 5(4) would confer
-- jurisdiction over a claim in the $20,000-to-$30,000 band EVEN IF the cause of
-- action accrued more than two years ago -- while a $15,000 claim of the same
-- age could not be heard on any terms.
--
-- THE CONTRARY READING IS THE MORE LIKELY ONE: s 5(3)(b) is a freestanding time
-- bar, s 5(4) is about value and nothing else, and the omission of subsection
-- (3) from s 5(4)'s opening words is loose drafting rather than design. Nothing
-- in the Act's structure suggests Parliament meant the parties to be able to
-- contract out of a limitation period by raising the amount in dispute.
--
-- The encoding states both readings and asserts where they diverge. It does not
-- choose. `the tribunal has jurisdiction` below follows the CONSERVATIVE reading,
-- because an encoding that anyone might rely on should not hand a claimant the
-- generous one.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tribunal has jurisdiction` IF
        `section 5(1) is satisfied` `the claim`
    AND NOT `section 5(2) excludes the claim` `the claim`
    AND `the value requirement is met` `the claim` `the limits`
    AND `the claim is within the two-year period in section 5(3)(b)` `the claim`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tribunal has jurisdiction on the literal reading of section 5(4)` IF
        `section 5(1) is satisfied` `the claim`
    AND NOT `section 5(2) excludes the claim` `the claim`
    AND (    `section 5(4) applies to the claim` `the claim` `the limits`
         OR  (    `the value requirement is met` `the claim` `the limits`
              AND `the claim is within the two-year period in section 5(3)(b)` `the claim`))

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the two readings of section 5 disagree about this claim` IF
        `the tribunal has jurisdiction on the literal reading of section 5(4)` `the claim` `the limits`
    AND NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `the claim` `the limits`

§§ `9 -- abandoning the excess, and what cannot be abandoned`

-- s 9(1): "Where the value of a claim exceeds the prescribed limit and NO
-- AGREEMENT IS REACHED between the parties ... under section 5(4), the claimant
-- MAY ABANDON THE EXCESS, and thereupon the tribunal has jurisdiction".
-- s 9(2): an order in relation to such a claim "is in FULL DISCHARGE of all
-- demands in respect of that claim."
--
-- Two things to notice.
--
-- First, s 9(2) says the order discharges all demands and does NOT say, as the
-- Employment Claims Act 2016 s 15(3) does, that the claimant cannot recover the
-- abandoned amount in any other court. The discharge is achieved through the
-- order rather than through an express bar, which comes to the same thing once
-- an order is made and to something different if none is.
--
-- Second, s 9 operates on an excess of VALUE over the limit. Where s 2(2)(a) or
-- (b) applies -- rescission, or a progress payment -- the value is the value of
-- the CONTRACT, and there is nothing for the claimant to abandon: reducing the
-- sum sought does not reduce the value of the contract. So a claim to rescind a
-- large contract is outside the tribunal absolutely, not merely capped.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is an excess the claimant could abandon under section 9` IF
        NOT `the value is within the prescribed limit` `the claim` `the limits`
    AND NOT `the claim`'s `the parties have agreed the extended limit by a memorandum signed by them`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `abandoning the sum sought would bring the value within the limit` IF
    CONSIDER `the claim`'s `the value`'s `the nature of the contract claim`
    WHEN `rescission of the contract` THEN NOT `the claim`'s `the value`'s `the claim relates to a contract`
    WHEN `recovery of a progress payment due under the contract` THEN NOT `the claim`'s `the value`'s `the claim relates to a contract`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the amount abandoned if the claimant abandons the excess` IS
    IF   `there is an excess the claimant could abandon under section 9` `the claim` `the limits`
    THEN `the value of the claim` (`the claim`'s `the value`) MINUS `the limits`'s `the prescribed limit`
    ELSE 0

§§ `6 -- one forum, and the gap in the way out of it`

-- s 6(1): "Where a claim has been lodged with a tribunal, NO PROCEEDINGS relating
-- to that claim may be brought before any other court EXCEPT --
--   (a) where the proceedings before that court were commenced BEFORE the claim
--       was lodged with the tribunal; or
--   (b) where the claim before the tribunal has been WITHDRAWN OR ABANDONED."
-- s 6(2): "No claim may be brought before a tribunal if proceedings relating to
--   that claim are PENDING IN or HAVE BEEN HEARD AND DETERMINED BY any other
--   court."
-- s 6(3): a claim is deemed lodged "when section 15 has been complied with."
--
-- THE GAP IS IN s 6(1)(b). The two exceptions are a claim withdrawn or abandoned
-- by the claimant, and proceedings elsewhere that were already running. Neither
-- covers a claim that the TRIBUNAL has discontinued under s 5(5) because it is
-- beyond the tribunal's jurisdiction.
--
-- So on the words: a claimant who lodges a claim the tribunal turns out not to
-- be able to hear, and whose proceedings the tribunal must therefore discontinue
-- under s 5(5), is barred by s 6(1) from bringing it anywhere else. s 46 does not
-- help, because s 46 is confined to "a claim that IS WITHIN THE JURISDICTION of a
-- tribunal".
--
-- Two things cure it in practice, and both depend on somebody acting before the
-- discontinuance:
--   * s 34 lets the claimant withdraw "at any time", which brings s 6(1)(b) into
--     play; and
--   * s 7 lets the tribunal TRANSFER the proceedings to the appropriate court
--     instead of discontinuing them, which preserves the claim.
--
-- The Employment Claims Act 2016, drafted thirty-two years later, has the same
-- structure in its s 16(2) and lists all three outcomes -- "withdrawn,
-- discontinued or dismissed for lack of jurisdiction". This Act lists two.
DECLARE WhatHappenedToTheTribunalClaim IS ONE OF
    `it is still live`
    `the claimant withdrew it under section 34`
    `the claimant abandoned it`
    `the tribunal discontinued it under section 5(5) as beyond its jurisdiction`
    `the tribunal transferred it to another court under section 7`

GIVEN `a claim was lodged with the tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `what happened` IS A WhatHappenedToTheTribunalClaim,
      `the other proceedings were commenced before the claim was lodged` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` IF
        `a claim was lodged with the tribunal`
    AND NOT `the other proceedings were commenced before the claim was lodged`
    AND CONSIDER `what happened`
        WHEN `the claimant withdrew it under section 34` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `the claimant abandoned it` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 46: "Nothing in this Act is to be construed as precluding a person from
-- lodging a claim THAT IS WITHIN THE JURISDICTION OF A TRIBUNAL in any other
-- court if that person ELECTS to institute proceedings in that other court".
--
-- The election is real and it is confined. It is available to a claimant whose
-- claim the tribunal could hear -- who may prefer the Magistrate's Court, and in
-- particular may have to, because the tribunal's clock runs for two years and
-- the ordinary one for six. It is not available to a claimant whose claim is
-- outside the tribunal's jurisdiction, which is the claimant who needs it.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 46 preserves an election to go elsewhere` IF
    `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `the claim` `the limits`

GIVEN `a claim was lodged with the tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `what happened` IS A WhatHappenedToTheTribunalClaim,
      `the other proceedings were commenced before the claim was lodged` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the claim is within the tribunal's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the claimant can still take the claim to another court` IF
        NOT `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` `a claim was lodged with the tribunal` `what happened` `the other proceedings were commenced before the claim was lodged`
    OR  CONSIDER `what happened`
        WHEN `the tribunal transferred it to another court under section 7` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `proceedings relating to the claim are pending in another court` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proceedings relating to the claim have been heard and determined by another court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 6(2) bars the claim from the tribunal` IF
        `proceedings relating to the claim are pending in another court`
    OR  `proceedings relating to the claim have been heard and determined by another court`

§§ `7, 8 and 10 -- transfer, division and a counterclaim that is too big`

-- s 7: "DESPITE SECTION 5, a tribunal may, at any time if it is of the opinion
-- that a claim ought to be dealt with by any other court, TRANSFER the
-- proceedings to that court whereupon the practice and procedure of that court
-- applies."
--
-- "Despite section 5" means the power survives the absence of jurisdiction, so
-- s 7 is the tribunal's alternative to discontinuing under s 5(5) -- and the only
-- one of the two that leaves the claimant with a claim.
--
-- s 8: "A claim must not be split or divided and pursued in separate proceedings
-- before a tribunal FOR THE SOLE PURPOSE of bringing the sum claimed in each of
-- such proceedings within the jurisdiction of a tribunal."
--
-- s 10(1): where a counterclaim is "a monetary claim which is NOT WITHIN the
-- jurisdiction of the tribunal", any party may apply to an appropriate court to
-- transfer the whole proceedings or the counterclaim.
-- s 10(2): the court may transfer the whole, keep the whole before the tribunal,
--   or split them.
-- s 10(4): "Where NO APPLICATION IS MADE under subsection (1) or where on such an
--   application it is ordered that the whole proceedings be heard before a
--   tribunal, THE TRIBUNAL HAS JURISDICTION to hear the proceedings DESPITE ANY
--   OTHER PROVISIONS OF THIS ACT."
--
-- s 10(4) is the widest jurisdictional provision in the Act. An out-of-limit
-- counterclaim that nobody applies to move leaves the tribunal with jurisdiction
-- over the whole of it, "despite any other provisions of this Act" -- so despite
-- the prescribed limit, despite the extended limit, and on the words despite the
-- two-year bar as well. A respondent with a $200,000 counterclaim who says
-- nothing hands the tribunal jurisdiction over it.
--
-- Note that s 10(4) is not confined, as s 35(2) is, by any cap on what the
-- tribunal may then order. s 35(2) caps "a money order or work order" at the
-- prescribed or extended limit and says nothing about a counterclaim within
-- s 10(4), which leaves the relationship between the two unresolved on the face
-- of the Act.
GIVEN `the counterclaim is a monetary claim outside the tribunal's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `an application was made under section 10(1)` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the court ordered the whole proceedings to be heard before the tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 10(4) gives the tribunal jurisdiction over the counterclaim` IF
        `the counterclaim is a monetary claim outside the tribunal's jurisdiction`
    AND (    NOT `an application was made under section 10(1)`
         OR  `the court ordered the whole proceedings to be heard before the tribunal`)

GIVEN `the claim was split into separate proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the sole purpose was to bring each within the tribunal's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 8 forbids the division` IF
        `the claim was split into separate proceedings`
    AND `the sole purpose was to bring each within the tribunal's jurisdiction`
